1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Imagine a country that
spends more than it collects.

2
00:00:03,295 --> 00:00:04,495
Not once.

3
00:00:04,646 --> 00:00:05,846
Every year.

4
00:00:06,334 --> 00:00:08,974
Hospitals, schools, pensions, roads.

5
00:00:09,054 --> 00:00:12,674
And every year, the money
runs out before the bills do.

6
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:14,797
So the government borrows.

7
00:00:15,217 --> 00:00:16,897
The next year, it borrows again.

8
00:00:17,297 --> 00:00:19,417
And again the year after that.

9
00:00:19,749 --> 00:00:22,229
Meanwhile, the old loans don't go away.

10
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:24,709
They come due, one after another.

11
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:27,310
So who keeps lending it the money?

12
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:31,650
And if the debt never seems to shrink,
how does any of this keep working?

13
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,760
Let's start with a country
where it stopped working.

14
00:00:37,421 --> 00:00:41,074
In 2009, Greece owed nearly
one hundred and thirty

15
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:44,221
percent of what its
economy produced in a year.

16
00:00:44,749 --> 00:00:46,029
Lenders panicked.

17
00:00:46,709 --> 00:00:50,549
Within two years, they were asking
for interest no government could pay.

18
00:00:51,226 --> 00:00:56,086
In 2012, Greece went through the biggest
government debt restructuring in history.

19
00:00:56,826 --> 00:00:58,926
Today, Greece owes even more.

20
00:00:59,426 --> 00:01:01,666
About one hundred and forty-six percent.

21
00:01:02,335 --> 00:01:06,055
And for most of the past year, it has
borrowed more cheaply than France.

22
00:01:06,935 --> 00:01:08,195
On ten-year bonds.

23
00:01:08,803 --> 00:01:10,003
Same country.

24
00:01:10,138 --> 00:01:11,158
More debt.

25
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:12,498
Cheaper money.

26
00:01:13,254 --> 00:01:16,094
So the size of the debt
isn't the whole story.

27
00:01:16,734 --> 00:01:19,894
What matters just as much
is who's lending, and why.

28
00:01:20,363 --> 00:01:22,043
So let's follow the money.

29
00:01:22,584 --> 00:01:26,064
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

30
00:01:26,684 --> 00:01:28,304
It really helps the channel.

31
00:01:30,674 --> 00:01:35,414
First, two words that get mixed
up all the time. Deficit and debt.

32
00:01:36,148 --> 00:01:38,228
Imagine a government
that collects one hundred

33
00:01:38,308 --> 00:01:42,285
billion in taxes, but
spends one hundred and ten.

34
00:01:42,365 --> 00:01:44,605
That gap of ten billion is the deficit.

35
00:01:44,905 --> 00:01:46,925
It's what happens in one year.

36
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:51,057
The debt is everything it still
owes from all the years before.

37
00:01:51,709 --> 00:01:54,849
A deficit is like water
running into a bathtub.

38
00:01:55,469 --> 00:01:57,949
The debt is the water already in it.

39
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:01,585
So a country can have a lot
of debt and no deficit at all.

40
00:02:01,685 --> 00:02:05,025
Or very little debt, and
a deficit every year.

41
00:02:07,505 --> 00:02:10,325
Let's see what that looks
like in two real countries.

42
00:02:11,185 --> 00:02:14,265
To compare them, we don't count
debt in kroner or dollars.

43
00:02:15,145 --> 00:02:18,885
We measure it against the size
of the whole economy: its GDP.

44
00:02:19,923 --> 00:02:22,183
A debt of a hundred billion
dollars would crush a

45
00:02:22,413 --> 00:02:25,883
small country, and barely
register in a huge one.

46
00:02:26,419 --> 00:02:28,099
Denmark first.

47
00:02:28,479 --> 00:02:33,979
At the end of 2025, its government owed
twenty-seven point nine percent of GDP.

48
00:02:34,643 --> 00:02:39,343
And in 2025, Denmark did what
governments supposedly never do.

49
00:02:39,903 --> 00:02:41,823
It collected more than it spent.

50
00:02:42,549 --> 00:02:44,509
A surplus of two point nine percent.

51
00:02:44,589 --> 00:02:47,109
And not for the first time.

52
00:02:47,382 --> 00:02:50,275
Since 2022, its debt has
fallen from thirty-three

53
00:02:50,505 --> 00:02:52,822
point three percent to under twenty-eight.

54
00:02:54,091 --> 00:02:56,631
A surplus can help reduce government debt.

55
00:02:56,711 --> 00:02:59,171
But it doesn't mean the debt disappears.

56
00:03:01,921 --> 00:03:04,101
Now let's look at the United States.

57
00:03:04,876 --> 00:03:08,316
In 2025, its total
government debt stood at

58
00:03:08,546 --> 00:03:11,516
roughly one hundred and
twenty-four percent of GDP.

59
00:03:12,017 --> 00:03:15,057
And it ran a deficit of about
six point eight percent.

60
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:18,037
That's a very different
picture from Denmark.

61
00:03:18,942 --> 00:03:21,882
The debt-to-GDP ratio can
fall even while the amount

62
00:03:22,112 --> 00:03:25,622
owed is still growing, if
the economy grows faster.

63
00:03:26,077 --> 00:03:29,877
But the percentage alone doesn't tell
us whether a debt is sustainable.

64
00:03:30,653 --> 00:03:32,933
For that, we need to
look at who owns it, what

65
00:03:33,163 --> 00:03:36,053
it costs to service, and
how the economy evolves.

66
00:03:38,743 --> 00:03:41,283
Now let's widen the picture across Europe.

67
00:03:41,996 --> 00:03:43,596
Estonia: twenty-four percent.

68
00:03:43,676 --> 00:03:45,806
Denmark: twenty-eight.

69
00:03:45,976 --> 00:03:47,826
Czech Republic: forty-four.

70
00:03:47,936 --> 00:03:50,713
Germany: sixty-three and a half.

71
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:55,493
France: one hundred and sixteen.
Italy: one hundred and thirty-seven.

72
00:03:55,573 --> 00:03:58,705
Greece: one hundred and forty-six.

73
00:03:59,005 --> 00:04:01,665
Six times as much, from
one end to the other.

74
00:04:01,745 --> 00:04:05,105
And every one of them still
owes money to someone.

75
00:04:07,695 --> 00:04:10,235
So who on earth is lending them the money?

76
00:04:11,085 --> 00:04:16,265
Governments don't usually walk into a
bank and ask for a loan. They sell bonds.

77
00:04:17,071 --> 00:04:18,991
A bond is a promise on paper.

78
00:04:19,711 --> 00:04:21,631
You give the government money today.

79
00:04:22,371 --> 00:04:25,691
It pays you interest, and gives
the money back on a fixed date.

80
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:29,580
Say you buy a ten-year bond
for one thousand dollars.

81
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:32,800
The government takes your
thousand and spends it.

82
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:35,690
You get interest payments every year, and

83
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:38,450
your thousand dollars
back in ten years' time.

84
00:04:39,082 --> 00:04:40,622
So who buys them?

85
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:42,953
Banks. Pension funds.

86
00:04:43,073 --> 00:04:48,753
Insurance companies. Investment funds.
Foreign governments. Central banks.

87
00:04:49,455 --> 00:04:51,795
And here's the part that's easy to miss.

88
00:04:52,217 --> 00:04:55,497
A pension fund doesn't hold
bonds for its own sake.

89
00:04:56,197 --> 00:04:59,177
It holds them for the people
who'll one day draw a pension.

90
00:04:59,652 --> 00:05:02,872
If you pay into a pension,
or keep savings in a fund,

91
00:05:02,952 --> 00:05:05,212
there's a good chance
you're one of the lenders.

92
00:05:06,041 --> 00:05:08,521
The government's debt
is somebody's savings.

93
00:05:09,281 --> 00:05:10,801
Very often, it's yours.

94
00:05:13,366 --> 00:05:15,266
And that brings us to Japan.

95
00:05:16,086 --> 00:05:21,766
Japan's government owes more than twice
its entire economy. Far more than Greece.

96
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:24,760
But about nine-tenths
of it is held at home.

97
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:28,380
By its banks, its insurers,
its pension funds.

98
00:05:28,802 --> 00:05:32,382
And the Bank of Japan, which
holds nearly half of its bonds.

99
00:05:32,965 --> 00:05:34,705
That changes the risks.

100
00:05:35,065 --> 00:05:37,025
But it doesn't make them disappear.

101
00:05:38,441 --> 00:05:41,201
Greece in 2009 was the opposite.

102
00:05:42,161 --> 00:05:44,701
Most of its debt was held
by foreign investors,

103
00:05:44,901 --> 00:05:47,561
many of them banks, who
could sell up and leave.

104
00:05:47,882 --> 00:05:49,642
And Greece used the euro.

105
00:05:50,202 --> 00:05:52,962
It couldn't create the money
its debts were written in.

106
00:05:53,548 --> 00:05:56,468
Today, more than half of Greek
debt is owed to other eurozone

107
00:05:56,698 --> 00:06:01,708
governments, through rescue funds,
on very long terms and at low rates.

108
00:06:02,369 --> 00:06:04,849
Same country. Different lenders.

109
00:06:05,449 --> 00:06:08,709
That's a big part of why it now
borrows more cheaply than France.

110
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:13,459
But that leaves the other question.

111
00:06:14,239 --> 00:06:17,479
If governments keep borrowing,
when do they ever pay it back?

112
00:06:18,341 --> 00:06:22,781
Imagine a government with one billion
dollars of bonds coming due this year.

113
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:25,441
It has to pay those lenders back.

114
00:06:25,581 --> 00:06:28,921
But it doesn't need to find
a billion dollars in taxes.

115
00:06:29,778 --> 00:06:35,238
It sells one billion dollars of new bonds,
and uses that money to repay the old ones.

116
00:06:35,890 --> 00:06:37,370
The old bonds are gone.

117
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:39,630
The lenders are paid in full.

118
00:06:39,710 --> 00:06:42,530
And the debt is exactly the same size.

119
00:06:43,265 --> 00:06:45,065
This is called refinancing.

120
00:06:45,145 --> 00:06:47,705
And governments do it all the time.

121
00:06:48,312 --> 00:06:52,292
Most governments refinance a
large part of their maturing debt.

122
00:06:52,732 --> 00:06:57,472
Individual bonds come due, but the
total debt doesn't have to reach zero.

123
00:06:58,095 --> 00:07:02,815
In 2015, Britain redeemed its last
four undated government bonds.

124
00:07:03,589 --> 00:07:08,669
Some of the historic liabilities behind
them could be traced back to 1720.

125
00:07:11,117 --> 00:07:13,217
But refinancing has a catch.

126
00:07:13,857 --> 00:07:17,117
It only works if somebody is
willing to buy the new bonds.

127
00:07:17,413 --> 00:07:20,273
And at an interest rate
the government can afford.

128
00:07:21,093 --> 00:07:24,013
When lenders get nervous,
they ask for more interest.

129
00:07:24,816 --> 00:07:27,576
Every cheap old bond that
comes due is replaced by an

130
00:07:27,806 --> 00:07:31,336
expensive new one, and the
interest bill starts to climb.

131
00:07:32,016 --> 00:07:34,756
A bigger interest bill
makes the deficit worse.

132
00:07:35,556 --> 00:07:38,056
A worse deficit makes
lenders more nervous.

133
00:07:38,391 --> 00:07:41,011
And nervous lenders ask for even more.

134
00:07:41,623 --> 00:07:43,763
That's the spiral Greece fell into.

135
00:07:44,303 --> 00:07:48,063
By early 2012, lenders wanted
around thirty percent a year.

136
00:07:48,563 --> 00:07:50,163
At times, far more.

137
00:07:50,747 --> 00:07:52,827
It ended the only way it could.

138
00:07:53,447 --> 00:07:56,467
Private lenders lost more than
half of what they were owed.

139
00:07:57,117 --> 00:08:01,717
Japan has sustained a much higher debt
ratio than Greece had before its crisis.

140
00:08:02,268 --> 00:08:06,328
But debt ratios alone don't tell us
when a country will get into trouble.

141
00:08:06,908 --> 00:08:10,428
What matters is whether lenders
believe they'll keep getting paid.

142
00:08:13,218 --> 00:08:16,098
So why don't governments
just pay everything off?

143
00:08:16,927 --> 00:08:18,447
Some have come close.

144
00:08:19,327 --> 00:08:22,507
In the early 2000s, Australia
was paying off its debt

145
00:08:22,737 --> 00:08:25,827
so fast it expected to be
clear within a few years.

146
00:08:26,133 --> 00:08:29,353
So it asked whether it would
still need to sell bonds at all.

147
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:32,040
It decided to keep selling them.

148
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:36,760
Not to pay for the budget, but because
financial markets relied on them.

149
00:08:36,993 --> 00:08:39,453
Futures contracts were tied to them, and

150
00:08:39,683 --> 00:08:42,433
traders used them to
manage interest rate risk.

151
00:08:43,083 --> 00:08:47,243
By 2007, what the government
owned outweighed what it owed.

152
00:08:48,003 --> 00:08:49,783
And it still kept selling bonds.

153
00:08:50,584 --> 00:08:54,244
So a government doesn't sell bonds
only because it's short of money.

154
00:08:55,024 --> 00:08:58,344
A working bond market can be
worth keeping for its own sake.

155
00:09:01,194 --> 00:09:03,634
So, who keeps lending
money to governments?

156
00:09:04,469 --> 00:09:08,669
Banks. Pension funds.
Insurers. Other governments.

157
00:09:08,769 --> 00:09:10,309
And, quite often, you.

158
00:09:10,842 --> 00:09:14,302
They can keep replacing maturing
debt with new borrowing.

159
00:09:14,662 --> 00:09:19,002
But that only works while they can
find lenders on terms they can afford.

160
00:09:20,329 --> 00:09:22,249
So next time you hear that a country owes

161
00:09:22,479 --> 00:09:25,509
trillions, don't just
ask how it will ever pay.

162
00:09:26,143 --> 00:09:28,043
Ask who's lending it the money.

163
00:09:28,463 --> 00:09:30,883
And whether they'll
still be there next year.

164
00:09:31,310 --> 00:09:34,050
Because government debt
is a chain of promises.

165
00:09:35,105 --> 00:09:37,545
It holds for as long as people believe it.

166
00:09:37,945 --> 00:09:39,215
Until they don't.
